From 7ba1db774853fc34b82b8add449f5007fc9e94b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Jeong, Heon" <blmarket@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:33:45 -0700
Subject: Fix memory leak of a command list parsed from a string

args_make_commands_now() takes a reference on the list it answers, which
is right for a command given in braces -- args_make_commands_prepare()
takes one for the state and args_make_commands_free() gives it back
again, so without the extra one the caller would hold none. A command
given as a string is parsed by args_make_commands(), and a parse result
already carries the caller's reference: load_cfg_from_buffer() queues one
and frees it once. Taking another leaves the list one reference above
zero for ever, so if-shell -F and confirm-before never free the commands
they were given as a string.

A configuration file of 20 "if-shell -F 1 'set -g @x 1'" lines exits
under AddressSanitizer with

    Direct leak of 304 byte(s) in 19 object(s) allocated from:
        #2 cmd_list_new cmd.c:600
        #3 cmd_parse_build_commands cmd-parse.y:901
        #4 cmd_parse_from_buffer cmd-parse.y:1057
        #5 cmd_parse_from_string cmd-parse.y:980
        #6 args_make_commands arguments.c:846
        #7 args_make_commands_now arguments.c:760

and none with this applied.

Applies to 3.7b at a different offset; the function is the same there.
---
 arguments.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arguments.c b/arguments.c
--- a/arguments.c
+++ b/arguments.c
@@ -749,8 +749,8 @@ args_make_commands_now(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item, u_int idx,
 	if (cmdlist == NULL) {
 		cmdq_error(item, "%s", error);
 		free(error);
 	}
-	else
+	else if (cmdlist == state->cmdlist)
 		cmdlist->references++;
 	args_make_commands_free(state);
 	return (cmdlist);
